# ── Bitbucket MCP server ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in one auth method. Every variable here can also live in
# ~/.config/bitbucket-mcp/config.json as a camelCase key (apiToken, allowWrites, …).
# The environment wins per FIELD, not per source, so a one-off BITBUCKET_ALLOW_WRITES=0
# overrides a config file that says true while leaving everything else in the file alone.
#
# Nothing here is required. With no credentials the server still starts and answers
# `bitbucket_auth_status` with setup instructions — a server that exits instead shows in
# the client as a bare "Connection closed" with its own explanation swallowed.

# ── Auth: pick ONE method ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Already signed in with the `bb` CLI? Skip this whole section. The server reads
# ~/.config/bb/hosts.yml, so `bb auth login` covers it — including an OAuth refresh
# token, which is why it shares the file rather than copying a token out of it.
#
# App passwords are NOT an option: Atlassian removed them on 2026-07-28.

# (A) Atlassian API token — simplest, and right if you only need to read.
#
# Create at https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens using
# "Create API token with scopes" and select Bitbucket as the app. A plain unscoped
# Atlassian token authenticates fine and then fails every Bitbucket call with
# "API Token provided has no Bitbucket scopes", which looks nothing like its cause.
#
# Set the email too. It is optional — without it the token is sent as a Bearer — but
# Bitbucket reports a scope problem specifically over Basic auth and generically over
# Bearer, so the email is the difference between a diagnosable failure and a puzzling one.
BITBUCKET_API_TOKEN=
BITBUCKET_EMAIL=

# (B) OAuth 2.0 browser login — a real login, refreshed automatically.
#
# Create a consumer under Workspace settings → Apps and features → OAuth consumers,
# and set its callback URL to exactly:
#
#     http://localhost:8724/callback
#
# The port is fixed and cannot vary: Bitbucket matches the callback against the value
# configured on the consumer, so a randomly chosen port can never be authorized.
#
# The secret is required, not optional. Bitbucket Cloud does not support PKCE, so this
# is a confidential-client flow and the secret is needed both to log in and to refresh
# afterwards.
#
# ⚠ Tick the permissions you want ON THE CONSUMER. Bitbucket ignores a scope request at
# login time, so this server cannot ask for what it needs — a consumer without
# `pullrequest:write` will answer 403 on every merge no matter what. For the write tools
# you want at least: account, repository, pullrequest:write, pipeline:write.
#
# Then call the `bitbucket_auth_login` tool, or run `bitbucket-mcp` and ask for it.
BITBUCKET_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
BITBUCKET_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# (C) A repository, project or workspace access token — for CI.
#
# These are not tied to an Atlassian account: `GET /user` returns 401, they appear in
# responses as a pseudo-user named after the token, and anything needing an identity is
# structurally unavailable. Scoped to whatever they were issued for.
BITBUCKET_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# Force the interpretation of a token rather than inferring it from which variable it
# arrived in. Rarely needed — `apiToken`, `accessToken` or `oauth`.
# BITBUCKET_AUTH_METHOD=

# ── Defaults ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Most tools take `workspace` and `repository` arguments; these are what they fall back
# to. Setting them is what lets a request say "list the open pull requests" without
# naming the repo every time.
#
# The workspace slug is the first path segment of a repo URL, not its display name.
# There is no way to search across workspaces — Atlassian removed both `GET /workspaces`
# and the cross-workspace repository listing, and they now return HTTP 410 — so
# `bitbucket_list_workspaces` is the only way to discover which ones you can reach.
BITBUCKET_WORKSPACE=
BITBUCKET_REPOSITORY=

# ── Safety ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Off by default. With this unset the mutating tools are not merely refused, they are
# NEVER REGISTERED — they do not appear in `tools/list` and cannot be called at all.
#
# Setting it to 1 adds eight tools: create / update / merge / decline / review / comment
# on a pull request, and run / stop a pipeline. The irreversible ones additionally
# require `confirm: true`, and a merge defaults to a dry run.
#
# Two of these are worth reading twice. Declining a pull request is IRREVERSIBLE on
# Bitbucket Cloud — there is no reopen endpoint, so a declined PR can only be replaced
# by a new one, losing its review history. And running a pipeline executes whatever
# `bitbucket-pipelines.yml` says, which may deploy.
BITBUCKET_ALLOW_WRITES=

# ── Optional tuning ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Retries on 429 and 5xx, honouring Retry-After. Defaults to 3.
# BITBUCKET_MAX_RETRIES=3

# Where the config file lives. Defaults to
# $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bitbucket-mcp/config.json, then ~/.config/bitbucket-mcp/config.json.
# BITBUCKET_CONFIG=

# Where to read a `bb` CLI login from. Defaults to $BB_CONFIG_DIR/hosts.yml, then
# ~/.config/bb/hosts.yml. Point it at a path that cannot exist to ignore the CLI login.
# BITBUCKET_HOSTS_FILE=

# Point at a different API root. Exists for testing against a local server.
# BITBUCKET_API_URL=https://api.bitbucket.org/2.0

# Print per-request debug lines to stderr. Never to stdout — that is the protocol channel.
# BITBUCKET_DEBUG=1
